          "verdict": "block",
          "summary": "The analysis is methodologically unsound: it is ungoverned (exploratory posture) and uses a negative binomial model without estimating the dispersion parameter, defaulting to alpha=1.0. Key model diagnostics are implausible (BIC negative large) and incomplete (no overdispersion test, no dispersion estimate). Variable definitions and cohort details are missing, preventing interpretation or replication. These issues block any credible claims until corrected.",
          "findings": [
            {
              "id": "ungoverned_analysis",
              "severity": "blocker",
              "category": "study_design",
              "title": "Analysis is exploratory, not bound to a pre-specified analysis plan",
              "evidenceRefs": [
                "stats-run.json > resultPosture.status: exploratory_standard_table"
              ],
              "whyItMatters": "The entire analysis is not governed by a preregistered method-selection or analysis specification, meaning results are likely cherry-picked and cannot support reliable inference. The manuscript presents it as a study, but internally it is labeled exploratory.",
              "actionableFix": "Bind the analysis to a valid method-selection artifact and analysis spec, and rerun under that governance before reporting as a study.",
              "reentryPoint": "protocol",
              "deterministicVerification": "Check stats-run.json > binding.status is not 'unbound'",
              "confidence": 0.95
            },
            {
              "id": "alpha_not_estimated",
              "severity": "blocker",
              "category": "method_choice",
              "title": "Negative binomial dispersion parameter alpha not estimated; default alpha=1.0 used",
              "evidenceRefs": [
                "stats-run.json > warnings: ValueWarning: Negative binomial dispersion parameter alpha not set. Using default value alpha=1.0."
              ],
              "whyItMatters": "The negative binomial model's key advantage is estimating overdispersion. Using a fixed alpha=1.0 fails to capture overdispersion correctly, leading to biased standard errors and invalid inference. The data might be overdispersed, but the model cannot adjust.",
              "actionableFix": "Re-run the model with alpha estimation enabled (e.g., in statsmodels, specify alpha parameter or use the appropriate estimator). Alternatively, justify why alpha=1.0 is appropriate (e.g., if data are Poisson).",
              "reentryPoint": "method_selection",
              "deterministicVerification": "Check that the final model output shows an estimated alpha value (not default 1.0) and that the warning about unset alpha does not appear.",
              "confidence": 0.95
